      "latency_ms": 45380.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1166,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen is, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons toward itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and other substances.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, a liquid, and a gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. The molecules move freely but are still attracted to one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, allowing insects to walk on water and for water to form droplets.\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport system in the human body (carrying nutrients) and in the environment (dissolving minerals in soil).\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant compound on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, rain, and underground aquifers."
